The question this campaign was testing is now answered. Question-answering finetuning can't train only the decoder: the compressor has to train too. Freezing the connector costs nothing. Nothing is left to launch, so I wrote `done` to NEXT.

## Results
Scores are the share of 1,500 questions per set that the Qwen3-8B judge marks correct (higher is better). Each number below is the arm minus the reference finetune with the same seed. The comparison is paired: both models answer the same questions.

| Arm (what stays frozen) | Documents | Photos | Text (MRQA) | Rule result |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Compressor + connector frozen (only the decoder LoRA trains), seeds 42 / 43 / 44 | −2.8 / −3.5 / −4.1 pt | ≈ 0 | −2.3 to −2.7 pt | **Failed** (earlier) |
| Compressor frozen; connector + decoder train, seeds 42 / 43 / 44 | −2.9 / −3.9 / **−3.5** pt | ≈ 0 | −2.3 / −3.1 / −0.7 pt | **Failed**: at seeds 43 and 44, below −3 pt with the 95% confidence interval entirely below zero |
| Connector frozen; compressor + decoder train, seeds 42 / 43 | **+2.3 / −0.6** pt | −0.6 / −1.3 pt | +0.1 / +0.3 pt | **Validated**: every set at least −2 pt at both seeds, with no confidence interval entirely below zero |

- **What it means:** the compressor's own finetuning carries about 3 points on documents and text. A cheap later stage that trains only the decoder on stored `<MEM>` vectors (the compressed slots the decoder reads) would lose those points. This matches the prediction written before these results.
- **Not triggered:** the rules kept the hand-off to build `<MEM>` caching for the case where decoder-only training passed. It didn't pass, so I didn't file it.
- **Connector-frozen recipe:** it scores the same as the full finetune but saves no time or memory (about 92 s per step and 34 GB, like the reference).
- **Checks:** the eval code was byte-for-byte the same (identical md5) on both sides of every comparison. All 12 new evals have 1,500 answers with no unreadable judge verdicts, and none ran on the excluded nodes. The frozen parts stayed unchanged in every full run.

## What I did this wake
- Recorded the judge scores and closing verdicts for every cell. Marked the prediction as confirmed in `campaign_freeze/CAMPAIGN.md`, rewrote `campaign_freeze/STATE.md`, and added ledger notes.
- Launched nothing.
- Filed a hand-off (43ec9b) to this project's chats:
  - commit the split-freeze code in `pisco/model.py` and `test_freeze.py`. It is still uncommitted, though the campaign's checkpoints were trained with it. The working tree matches the saved patch `campaign_freeze/patches/split_freeze.diff`.
  - write the result into `docs/training_plan.md` and HANDOFF.md thread 7. Proposed text is in STATE.md.
- The results page still says "0 table rows". That bug is already reported to expmon, so I didn't report it again.

## Your decision
Two optional follow-ups. Both need a new campaign, because this campaign's rules forbid changing the data or the mission:
1. **Train once, then freeze:** take the reference finetune's compressor, which has already had one round of question-answering training. Freeze it, and train only the decoder on new data, compared with a full finetune on that data. This campaign only showed that the compressor needs question-answering training once. It did not show it must train on every later dataset.
2. **Make connector-frozen the default recipe:** it is as good as the full finetune and has one fewer part that moves, but it is not faster.

The hand-off to commit the code and update the docs is waiting for this project's chats.
